{"maxId":265,"entries":{"chapter-77-study-cardiac-arrhythmias|Chapter 77 Study: Cardiac Arrhythmias":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"copy|Sharp rise in syncope incidence after age 70 (p1193); worse outcomes (higher hospitalization/death) than in younger patients.":["u3"],"copy|Orthostatic hypotension (OH): prevalence 30% (p1193) in those >75y, up to 50% (p1193) in frail nursing-home residents. Defined as SBP drop ≥ 20 mmHg (p1193) or DBP drop ≥ 10 mmHg (p1193) on standing. Four subtypes (p1194): initial (within 15s), classic (within 3 min), delayed (>3 min), neurogenic (autonomic-failure-driven).":["u4"],"copy|Vasovagal syncope (VVS) has three response types (p1194): cardioinhibitory = pause ≥ 3 s (p1194) or HR < 40 bpm (p1194) for more than 10s; vasodepressor = SBP falls ≥ 50 mmHg without symptoms or 30–50 mmHg with symptoms, HR fall ≤ 10% (Table 77-2, p1194); mixed. Dual-chamber pacing reasonable for age > 40y (p1194) with recurrent VVS and spontaneous pauses.":["u5"],"copy|Carotid sinus syndrome: CSH prevalence up to 30% (p1195) among older adults with unexplained falls; CSS = ≥ 3-second (p1195) pause or SBP drop ≥ 50 mmHg (p1195) on carotid sinus massage, plus symptoms.":["u6"],"copy|Two mechanisms: sinus node dysfunction (SND) and atrioventricular block (AVB) (p1195).":["u7"],"copy|SND (historically \"sick sinus syndrome\"): sinus bradycardia, sinus arrest, or SA exit block (p1195); tachy-brady syndrome common in older SND patients.":["u8"],"copy|More than 80% (p1193) of permanent pacemakers (PPM) are placed in patients 65 years or older (p1193); SND is the leading indication for PPM in this age group.":["u9"],"copy|AVB has three degrees (p1198) — first degree (PR > 200 ms (p1198)), second degree (Mobitz I/Wenckebach vs Mobitz II), third degree/complete.":["u10"],"copy|PPM indications (Tables 77-3/77-4, p1197/p1199): symptomatic SND or symptomatic Mobitz II/high-grade/3rd-degree AVB, if not attributable to reversible causes or caused by medication required for other conditions; device selection — SND: single-chamber atrial if no AV conduction abnormality, dual-chamber if present; AVB: single-chamber ventricular if infrequent pacing expected.Current practice (not the book): in acquired Mobitz II, high-grade or third-degree AV block not caused by reversible or physiologic causes, permanent pacing is recommended regardless of symptoms — ACC/AHA/HRS bradycardia guideline, 2018.":["u11"],"copy|CRT highest-likelihood-to-respond criteria (Table 77-5, p1199, image): LVEF ≤ 35% (p1199, image), sinus rhythm, LBBB, QRS ≥ 150 ms (p1199, image), NYHA II/III/ambulatory IV. A separate AVB-driven pathway (in running text, not the table) favors CRT over conventional RV pacing when LVEF < 50% (p1199) and ventricular pacing is expected > 40% (p1199) of the time — a different LVEF cutoff from the HF/dyssynchrony CRT indication above.":["u12"],"copy|Mode Selection Trial: RV pacing ≥ 40% (p1199) of the time → 2.6-fold (p1199) increase in HF hospitalizations.":["u13"],"copy|TAVR: new LBBB in 19–55% (p1200), high-degree AVB in 10% (p1200) after TAVR; up to 30% (p1200) of new-LBBB patients have their first high-degree AVB episode after discharge.":["u14"],"copy|Rate control target HR 80–110 bpm (p1201); β-blockers/non-dihydropyridine CCBs first-line, but CCBs are related to higher mortality when LVEF <40%; digoxin less effective but can be considered (caution: narrow therapeutic index).":["u15"],"copy|AAD choice for AF rhythm control depends on comorbidity (Table 77-6, p1201): no heart disease → class Ic or III; CAD → class Ic contraindicated, class III OK; reduced EF/HF → amiodarone and dofetilide can be used (dronedarone & class Ic contraindicated).":["u16"],"copy|AFFIRM trial: AAD therapy associated with higher all-cause mortality in the subgroup older than 70 (p1201).":["u17"],"copy|CABANA trial (n = 2204 (p1201)) patients: catheter ablation not superior to AAD for the primary composite endpoint at 5y, but reduced AF recurrence and the combined death/CV-hospitalization secondary endpoint.":["u18"],"copy|Stroke risk is 5× higher (p1202) with AF. CHA2DS2-VASc: anticoagulate if ≥ 2 (p1203) points in men or ≥ 3 (p1203) in women (Table 77-8); maximum score 9 (p1202). HAS-BLED: 0–2 (p1202) score = low risk, 3–9 (p1202) score = high risk.":["u19"],"copy|DOACs preferred over warfarin as first-line stroke prevention for nonvalvular AF — valvular AF (moderate–severe MS or mechanical valve) needs warfarin regardless of score; no dabigatran/rivaroxaban/edoxaban in ESRD/dialysis (Table 77-8, p1203); apixaban and edoxaban show lower major-bleeding risk in patients 75 years or older (p1202), while full-dose dabigatran and rivaroxaban carry increased GI bleeding risk (PPI recommended).":["u20"],"copy|WATCHMAN: the original trials used warfarin to INR 2–3 with aspirin for approximately 45 days. Reassessment by TEE looked for device thrombus and significant peri-device leak before transition to antiplatelet therapy. Current postimplant regimens depend on device, bleeding risk, imaging and labeling (p1203).":["u21"],"copy|Dabigatran. RE-LY studied 150 mg and 110 mg twice daily: 150 mg reduced stroke/systemic embolism by 34% with similar major bleeding to warfarin, while 110 mg had similar efficacy and about 20% less major bleeding. The 110 mg twice-daily AF regimen is jurisdiction-specific (for example, European labeling in selected older patients or with verapamil) and is not the US adult AF dose. Dose by local labeling and renal function. Specific reversal agent: idarucizumab (Table 77-9, p1204).":["u22"],"copy|Rivaroxaban. In ROCKET-AF, 20 mg daily (15 mg for CrCl 30–49 mL/min) was noninferior to warfarin for stroke prevention, with a similar major-bleeding rate. US adult AF labeling uses 20 mg with the evening meal when CrCl >50 mL/min, or 15 mg with the evening meal when CrCl ≤50 mL/min within labeled renal-use conditions. US commercial sales of andexanet alfa ended in December 2025. Management of life-threatening bleeding follows local reversal protocols (Table 77-9, p1204).":["u23"],"copy|Apixaban for AF: 5 mg twice daily, reduced to 2.5 mg twice daily when at least two criteria apply: age ≥80 years, weight ≤60 kg, serum creatinine ≥1.5 mg/dL. ARISTOTLE found 21% less stroke/systemic embolism and 31% less major bleeding versus warfarin (Table 77-9, p1205, Eliquis prescribing information).Current practice (not the book): the Eliquis label's weight criterion is body weight ≤60 kg (not <60) — Eliquis prescribing information §2.1 (BMS).":["u24"],"copy|Edoxaban. In the US, adult nonvalvular AF dosing is 60 mg once daily for CrCl >50 to 95 mL/min, or 30 mg once daily for CrCl 15–50 mL/min. Avoid use for NVAF when CrCl exceeds 95 mL/min. Weight and P-gp criteria in Table 77-9 pertain to trial protocols and some other indications or jurisdictions. ENGAGE AF studied higher- and lower-dose strategies, both noninferior for stroke/systemic embolism in its primary analysis, with less major bleeding. The low-dose arm had more ischemic stroke than warfarin (p1205, Savaysa prescribing information §2.1).":["u25"],"copy|Atrial flutter: cavotricuspid-isthmus ablation success > 90–95% (p1206), complications < 1–2% (p1206).":["u26"],"copy|SVT: AVNRT is the most common type in older adults. β-blockers and CCBs are first-line therapy (p1206); acute options in Table 77-10 include vagal maneuvers and adenosine, with synchronized cardioversion if hemodynamically unstable or refractory to adenosine (Table 77-10, p1206); ablation success 85% (p1206) to better than 95% (p1206), major complications < 2–3% (p1206).":["u27"],"copy|Primary-prevention ICD: on GDMT, ischemic cardiomyopathy with LVEF ≤35% and NYHA II–III, or LVEF ≤30% and NYHA I, after the applicable waiting periods (at least 40 days after MI and reassessment after revascularization, generally at least 90 days). Nonischemic cardiomyopathy may qualify with LVEF ≤35% and NYHA II–III despite GDMT. Expected meaningful survival should exceed one year (p1206, AHA/ACC/HFSA).":["u28"],"copy|ICD secondary prevention: cardiac arrest from VT/VF not from a reversible cause; spontaneous sustained VT with structural heart disease; or unexplained syncope with inducible hemodynamically significant VT/VF at EPS when drug therapy is ineffective, not tolerated, or not preferred (p1206).":["u29"],"copy|PPM deactivation can be decided by the patient or legal surrogate via shared decision-making; after that, a written request to deactivate the PPM plus a DNR order is required (p1200). For ICDs, the patient or legal surrogate should be informed of the right to deactivate when the end-of-life decision is appropriate (p1207).":["u30"],"copy|PPM-dependent patients may die immediately on deactivation; non-dependent patients need monitoring for symptoms (p1200).":["u31"],"copy|The CHA2DS2-VASc \"adjusted stroke risk\" table (Table 77-7A, p1202, image) is non-monotonic as printed, and dips twice: score 6 → 9.8 %/year, score 7 → 9.6 %/year (a small dip), then score 8 → 6.7 %/year (a bigger dip, equal to score 5) — reproduced exactly as in the book; don't assume risk rises smoothly with each added point, and don't assume the 7→8 step is the only place it falls.":["u32"],"copy|Two different LVEF thresholds trigger CRT in this chapter: ≤ 35% (p1199, image, Table 77-5) for the classic HF/dyssynchrony indication vs < 50% (p1199) for the AVB-driven \"expected to need >40% ventricular pacing\" pathway (running text) — easy to conflate on a question stem.":["u33"],"copy|Not all DOACs reduce bleeding vs warfarin: apixaban and edoxaban show less major bleeding at their studied doses; dabigatran only at 110 mg (150 mg shows similar bleeding to warfarin, not less); rivaroxaban (20 mg, the dose studied) shows only a similar major-bleeding rate to warfarin (p1204, image).":["u34"],"recall|Recall":["u35"],"copy|CHAPTER 77s / HAZZARD":["u36"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 77; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u37"],"copy|Syncope in older adults":["u38"],"copy|Bradyarrhythmia":["u39"],"copy|Atrial fibrillation (AF)":["u40"],"copy|DOACs (Table 77-9, p1204–1205 — table reproduced as a page image; percentages below verified directly against that image, not text-searchable)":["u41"],"copy|Other tachyarrhythmias":["u42"],"copy|End-of-life device management":["u43"],"copy|Exam traps / book notes":["u44"],"copy|1.":["u45"],"copy|In a nursing-home resident, orthostatic hypotension is defined by what blood-pressure drop, and how prevalent is it in frail older nursing-home residents?":["u46"],"copy|SBP drop ≥ 20 mmHg or DBP drop ≥ 10 mmHg on standing; up to 50% of frail nursing-home residents (p1193).":["u47"],"copy|2.":["u48"],"copy|Name the four subtypes of orthostatic hypotension.":["u49"],"copy|Initial (immediate, within 15 s), classic (within 3 min), delayed (>3 min), and neurogenic (autonomic-nervous-system dysfunction) (p1194).":["u50"],"copy|3.":["u51"],"copy|What defines the cardioinhibitory type of vasovagal response, and at what age is dual-chamber pacing considered reasonable for recurrent vasovagal syncope with spontaneous pauses?":["u52"],"copy|A cardioinhibitory vasovagal response may produce a pause ≥3 seconds or a heart rate <40/min for more than 10 seconds.":["u53"],"copy|Dual-chamber pacing may be reasonable in selected patients over 40 with recurrent vasovagal syncope and documented prolonged spontaneous pauses (p1194).":["u53"],"copy|4.":["u54"],"copy|What carotid sinus massage finding defines carotid sinus hypersensitivity, and what additional feature makes it carotid sinus syndrome?":["u55"],"copy|A pause ≥ 3 seconds or a systolic BP drop ≥ 50 mmHg during carotid sinus massage; carotid sinus syndrome requires that this be accompanied by symptoms of syncope or presyncope (p1195).":["u56"],"copy|5.":["u57"],"copy|What proportion of permanent pacemakers are placed in patients 65 or older, and what is the leading indication in this age group?":["u58"],"copy|More than 80%; sinoatrial (sinus node) dysfunction is the leading indication (p1193).":["u59"],"copy|6.":["u60"],"copy|What are the highest-likelihood-to-respond criteria for cardiac resynchronization therapy?":["u61"],"copy|LVEF ≤ 35%, sinus rhythm, LBBB, QRS ≥ 150 ms, NYHA class II, III, or ambulatory IV (p1199).":["u62"],"copy|8.":["u63"],"copy|After TAVR, what percentage of patients develop new LBBB, and what percentage develop high-degree AV block?":["u64"],"copy|New LBBB in 19% to 55%; high-degree AVB in 10% (p1200).":["u65"],"copy|10.":["u66"],"copy|How many patients were randomized in the CABANA trial, and was catheter ablation superior to antiarrhythmic drugs for the primary composite endpoint?":["u67"],"copy|2,204 patients; catheter ablation was NOT superior to AAD for the primary endpoint (all-cause mortality, disabling stroke, serious bleeding, or cardiac arrest) at 5 years, though it did reduce AF recurrence and the combined death/cardiovascular-hospitalization secondary endpoint (p1201).":["u68"],"copy|11.":["u69"],"copy|What CHA2DS2-VASc score threshold triggers anticoagulation in men versus women, and what is the maximum possible score?":["u70"],"copy|≥ 2 in men or ≥ 3 in women (p1203, Table 77-8); maximum score is 9 (p1202, Table 77-7A).":["u71"],"copy|12.":["u72"],"copy|On the HAS-BLED score, what ranges represent low versus high bleeding risk?":["u73"],"copy|0–2 = low risk; 3–9 = high risk (p1202).":["u74"],"copy|13.":["u75"],"copy|Which two DOACs show a lower major-bleeding risk than warfarin in patients 75 years or older, and which two carry an increased risk of GI bleeding at full dose?":["u76"],"copy|Apixaban and edoxaban show lower major bleeding; full-dose dabigatran and rivaroxaban are associated with increased GI bleeding risk (a PPI is recommended with these two) (p1202).":["u77"],"copy|14.":["u78"],"copy|What was the initial warfarin regimen in the original WATCHMAN studies, and what should follow-up imaging assess before changing antithrombotic therapy?":["u79"],"copy|The historical regimen used warfarin INR 2–3 with aspirin for about 45 days.":["u80"],"copy|Before switching to the planned antiplatelet regimen, assess device-related thrombus and peri-device leak.":["u80"],"copy|Current regimens and imaging follow-up depend on device labeling and the patient's bleeding/thrombotic risks (p1203).":["u80"],"copy|15.":["u81"],"copy|What are the primary-prevention ICD criteria for ischemic cardiomyopathy, including the required time from MI or revascularization?":["u82"],"copy|After MI, primary-prevention ICD criteria include LVEF ≤35% with NYHA II–III, or LVEF ≤30% with NYHA I, despite guideline-directed therapy and with expected meaningful survival beyond one year.":["u83"],"copy|Assess at least 40 days after MI and reassess after revascularization, generally at least 90 days afterward (p1206, AHA/ACC/HFSA).":["u83"],"copy|Answer":["u84","u85","u86","u87","u88","u89","u90","u91","u92","u93","u94","u95","u96"],"copy|DOACs preferred over warfarin as first-line stroke prevention for nonvalvular AF — valvular AF (moderate–severe MS or mechanical valve) needs warfarin regardless of score; no dabigatran/rivaroxaban/edoxaban in ESRD/dialysis (Table 77-8, p1203); apixaban and edoxaban show lower major-bleeding risk in patients 75 years or older (p1202), while full-dose dabigatran and rivaroxaban carry increased GI bleeding risk (p1202 recommends a PPI with these agents). In clinical practice, individualize gastroprotection according to overall gastrointestinal bleeding risk. ESC 2024 AF guideline, §6.7.1.":["u20"],"copy|Apixaban and edoxaban show lower major bleeding; full-dose dabigatran and rivaroxaban are associated with increased GI bleeding risk (p1202).":["u77"],"copy|Hazzard recommends a PPI with these agents.":["u77"],"copy|The 2024 ESC guideline individualizes gastroprotection according to gastrointestinal bleeding risk.":["u77"],"copy|DOACs preferred over warfarin as first-line stroke prevention for nonvalvular AF — valvular AF (moderate–severe MS or mechanical valve) needs warfarin regardless of score; no dabigatran/rivaroxaban/edoxaban in ESRD/dialysis (Table 77-8, p1203); apixaban and edoxaban show lower major-bleeding risk in patients 75 years or older (p1202), while full-dose dabigatran and rivaroxaban carry increased GI bleeding risk (p1202 recommends a PPI with these agents).Current practice (not the book): individualize gastroprotection according to overall gastrointestinal bleeding risk. ESC 2024 AF guideline, §6.7.1.":["u20"],"copy|WATCHMAN: p1203 describes warfarin to INR 2–3 for 45 days. If TEE confirms complete LAA closure, stop warfarin and use aspirin plus clopidogrel for 6 months, then long-term aspirin. For high bleeding risk, the chapter describes six months of clopidogrel plus long-term aspirin without oral anticoagulation.":["u255"],"copy|Dabigatran: Table 77-9 gives 150 mg twice daily or 110 mg twice daily with concomitant verapamil, age ≥80, or increased GI-bleeding risk. In RE-LY, 150 mg reduced stroke/systemic embolism by 34% with similar major bleeding to warfarin; 110 mg had similar efficacy and about 20% less major bleeding. The specific reversal agent is idarucizumab (p1204).Current practice (not the book): 110 mg twice daily for AF is a jurisdiction-dependent regimen, including in European prescribing information; the US adult AF label does not include this regimen — EMA Pradaxa product information.":["u22"],"copy|Rivaroxaban: Table 77-9 gives 20 mg daily, or 15 mg daily at CrCl 30–49 mL/min. ROCKET-AF found noninferior stroke prevention and a similar major-bleeding rate versus warfarin. The book lists andexanet alfa (Andexxa) for reversal (p1204).Current practice (not the book): US NVAF labeling gives 20 mg with the evening meal for CrCl >50 mL/min or 15 mg with the evening meal for CrCl ≤50 mL/min within labeled renal-use conditions. US sales of Andexxa ended December 22, 2025 — Xarelto prescribing information, FDA Andexxa safety communication.":["u23"],"copy|Apixaban for AF: 5 mg twice daily, reduced to 2.5 mg twice daily when at least two criteria apply: age ≥80 years, weight <60 kg, serum creatinine ≥1.5 mg/dL. ARISTOTLE found 21% less stroke/systemic embolism and 31% less major bleeding versus warfarin (Table 77-9, p1205).Current practice (not the book): the Eliquis label's weight criterion is body weight ≤60 kg (not <60) — Eliquis prescribing information §2.1 (BMS).":["u24"],"copy|Edoxaban: Table 77-9 gives 60 mg once daily, reduced to 30 mg once daily for at least one of: CrCl 30–50 mL/min, weight ≤60 kg, or concurrent cyclosporine, dronedarone, erythromycin or ketoconazole (p1205). ENGAGE AF found both dose strategies noninferior for stroke/systemic embolism in the primary analysis, with less major bleeding.Current practice (not the book): US adult NVAF labeling gives 60 mg daily for CrCl >50 to 95 mL/min or 30 mg daily for CrCl 15–50 mL/min. Avoid use for NVAF with CrCl >95 mL/min — Savaysa prescribing information, §2.1.":["u256"],"copy|Primary-prevention ICD (p1206): on GDMT, ischemic cardiomyopathy >40 days after MI or >90 days after revascularization with LVEF ≤35% and NYHA II–III, or LVEF <30% and NYHA I. For nonischemic cardiomyopathy: LVEF ≤35% with NYHA II–III despite GDMT. A third indication is prior MI with NSVT, EF ≤40% and inducible sustained monomorphic VT on electrophysiologic study.":["u28"],"copy|Not all DOACs reduce bleeding vs warfarin: apixaban and edoxaban show less major bleeding at their studied doses; dabigatran only at 110 mg (150 mg shows similar bleeding to warfarin, not less); rivaroxaban (20 mg, the dose studied) shows only a similar major-bleeding rate to warfarin (Table 77-9, pp1204–1205).":["u34"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): for AF with stable coronary disease more than one year after revascularization or CAD not requiring revascularization, oral anticoagulation alone is recommended over adding antiplatelet therapy when there is no history of stent thrombosis.":["u257"],"copy|Arrange follow-up after AF during acute illness — ACC/AHA/ACCP/HRS AF guideline, §§6.8.2 and 10.10.":["u128"],"copy|Actual risk at a given score varies among populations (pp1202–1203).":["u135"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): the 2024 ESC guideline uses CHA₂DS₂-VA without sex: it recommends anticoagulation at two or more points and consideration at one.":["u136"],"copy|Keep the scoring system specified by the question.":["u137"],"copy|Dofetilide is the choice offered in the separate case with ischemic cardiomyopathy and EF 35% (Table 77-6, p1201).":["u169"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): dofetilide initiation requires renal dosing and inpatient QT monitoring; amiodarone warrants surveillance for extracardiac toxicity.":["u170"],"copy|Avoid dronedarone after recent decompensation or with advanced symptomatic heart failure.":["u171"],"copy|The chapter describes rate control for older patients with mild symptoms and notes that an AFFIRM analysis in patients older than 70 associated antiarrhythmic therapy with higher all-cause mortality.":["u176"],"copy|Electrical cardioversion can restore sinus rhythm.":["u258"],"copy|Catheter ablation is more effective than drugs at suppressing AF, but the need for stroke prevention still depends on stroke risk.":["u179"],"copy|CABANA did not show superiority for its primary composite endpoint in the intention-to-treat comparison (pp1200–1202).":["u180"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): rhythm control early after diagnosis and rhythm control in selected patients with heart failure can improve outcomes.":["u177"],"copy|Consider symptoms, treatment tolerance, stage of AF and patient preferences — ACC/AHA/ACCP/HRS AF guideline, 2023, §8.1.":["u178"],"copy|The book specifies >40 days after MI or >90 days after revascularization.":["u196"],"copy|On GDMT the ischemic threshold is EF ≤35% with NYHA II–III or EF <30% with NYHA I.":["u259"],"copy|A further indication is prior MI with NSVT, EF ≤40% and inducible sustained monomorphic VT on electrophysiologic study (p1206).":["u260"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): the guideline uses at least 40 days after MI and at least 90 days after revascularization, with LVEF ≤30% in NYHA I; anticipated meaningful survival exceeds one year — AHA/ACC/HRS ICD decision aid.":["u203"],"copy|An indication for pacing with substantial anticipated RV pacing and impaired EF is a different CRT pathway from symptomatic heart failure with LBBB and a broad QRS (pp1197–1200, Tables 77-3 to 77-5).":["u221"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): after TAVR, new AV block with persistent symptoms or hemodynamic instability warrants permanent pacing before discharge; other new conduction disturbances may warrant surveillance — ACC/AHA/HRS bradycardia guideline, §8.1.2.4.":["u261"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): the 2024 ESC guideline individualizes gastroprotection according to gastrointestinal bleeding risk.":["u77"],"copy|Hazzard gives warfarin to INR 2–3 for 45 days, then discontinues warfarin if TEE confirms complete LAA closure.":["u262"],"copy|Follow with aspirin and clopidogrel for 6 months, then long-term aspirin (p1203).":["u263"],"copy|After MI, the book gives >40 days after MI or >90 days after revascularization and, on GDMT, EF ≤35% with NYHA II–III or EF <30% with NYHA I.":["u264"],"copy|A separate indication after MI is NSVT with EF ≤40% and inducible sustained monomorphic VT on electrophysiologic study (p1206).":["u265"]},"sequenceOffsets":[[266,-169],[293,-168],[296,-167],[302,-163],[332,-159],[334,-151],[347,-150],[353,-149],[364,-144],[365,-142],[397,0]]}
